# <<[ package_name ]>>

Generated [% if backend_language == "go" %]Go 1.26, chi, Huma, React, and OpenAPI[% else %]Rust, Batter, Axum, React, and OpenAPI[% endif %] workspace.

[% if backend_language != "go" %]
The `batter` facade is a pinned Git dependency in `Cargo.toml` (not a registry
release) that selects only the features this workspace uses: `axum`[% if db == "postgres" %], `sqlx`[% endif %][% if metrics == "otlp" %], `otlp`[% endif %][% if jobs == "runledger" %], `runledger`[% endif %].
[% if db == "postgres" %]
Database tests also pin `postgres-test-harness` to the revision that Batter's SQLx
fixture support selects; upgrade both pins together.
[% endif %]
The services target Unix and require network access on the first build. Commit
`Cargo.lock` after bootstrap; upgrade the Batter revision deliberately.

`crates/<<[ package_name ]>>-runtime` owns protected startup, native signals, and
resource cleanup through `batter::service::start`. `Startup::scoped` constrains
initialization to stage metadata, cleanup reservation, and supported registration
adapters. Startup has a shared 30-second budget, including database connection and
migrations when enabled. HTTP handlers have a 10-second response construction
budget; streaming bodies and WebSockets need separate limits. Shutdown drains for
10 seconds, then allows 2 seconds for cancellation and 1 second for abort/reap
before separately bounded cleanup. These policies are application-owned. Failed or
incomplete cleanup makes the process exit unsuccessfully.

Each HTTP surface assembles through Batter's sealed `HttpBoundary`: server
correlation and one HTTP observer are outermost, probes sit outside admission, and
lifecycle admission plus the request deadline wrap every documented route and the
fallback. OpenAPI routers join through a route inventory derived from their own
documents, so undocumented routes are never served. Handlers read the request's
operation context and server-generated correlation ID from `AdmittedRequest`;
inbound `x-request-id` headers are replaced. Shutdown phases share one first-stop
clock; scheduling delay consumes the allowance. `jig dev` has a shorter,
two-second SIGTERM grace period before force-killing the service process group.
Slow requests can therefore be interrupted before Batter finishes draining or
closing resources. To test the full shutdown budget, launch the built service binary
directly and send it SIGTERM; production process managers must allow enough time
for drain, cancellation, abort/reap, and cleanup.
[% if db != "none" %]
Database setup uses Batter's finite `Command` owner with 30 seconds for work and
independently bounded pool cleanup, including after SIGINT/SIGTERM or migration
failure. Migrations run on one leased connection that is retired on failure or
cancellation. Cancellation does not roll back database creation or committed
migrations.
[% endif %]

Public `/health/live` and `/health/ready` probes remain outside request admission.
The separate admin service also exposes those operational probes; admin business
routes remain denied until an application authorizer is supplied. Do not add business
data to unauthenticated probes.
[% if db != "none" %]Readiness combines the runtime lifecycle, a supervised PostgreSQL health
monitor that probes the pool every five seconds, and the application's
`application-state` readiness condition. A database outage turns readiness to 503
within about ten seconds and recovers without a restart, while liveness stays 200.
PostgreSQL pool construction registers local cleanup before an explicit bounded
connectivity check; local closure does not roll back remote transactions or
guarantee remote session termination.[% else %]Readiness combines the runtime lifecycle with the application's
`application-state` readiness condition.[% endif %] Configuration is captured from the
environment once into Batter settings;[% if db != "none" %] `DATABASE_URL` is a redacted secret, and[% endif %] settings errors name the variable without echoing its value. Change lifecycle budgets
in the runtime crate and request policy in
`crates/<<[ package_name ]>>-http-common/src/requests.rs`; these are source policies,
not `.jig.toml` settings.
[% if metrics == "otlp" %]

### Metrics export

Set `METRICS_OTLP_ENDPOINT` to an HTTP(S) URL ending in `/v1/metrics` to export
Batter's bounded foundation metrics catalog (operations, admission decisions, task
exits, cleanup, and shutdown) over OTLP/HTTP every ten seconds, with a bounded final
export after shutdown. Without it nothing is installed and no collector is
contacted. While export is enabled, ambient `OTEL_*` variables are rejected because
the native builders would merge them. Export diagnostics are logged and never
change the process exit code.
[% endif %]
[% if jobs == "runledger" %]

### Background jobs

`crates/<<[ package_name ]>>-jobs` holds Runledger job handlers, the job catalog,
worker settings, and enqueue helpers. The public API process runs the worker as a
Batter-supervised component: startup waits for native initialization, drain stops
claiming work, and the database pool closes only after the worker settles. The
admin API does not run a worker. Runledger's migration history is applied before
the application's, in the same `_sqlx_migrations` table. Set `JOBS_WORKER_ID` for a
stable worker identity; otherwise each process derives a unique one. Enqueue
jobs with `batter::runledger::run_atomic` together with the application writes
they depend on.
[% endif %]
[% endif %]

## Quickstart

Prerequisites: [% if backend_language == "go" %]Go 1.26 or newer[% else %]Rust 1.94 or newer[% endif %], Node <<[ node_version ]>> or newer, and <<[ package_manager ]>> (`<<[ package_manager_spec ]>>`).[% if db == "postgres" %] Disposable PostgreSQL integration tests also require Docker.[% endif %]

```sh
<<[ web_run_command ]>> bootstrap
scripts/jig setup
```

`<<[ web_run_command ]>> bootstrap` runs `scripts/jig bootstrap`: it fetches backend dependencies and installs every frontend workspace dependency[% if backend_language == "go" %], then prepares sqlc and API client code from the local schemas[% endif %]. A fresh init intentionally has no lockfile, so do not start with `<<[ web_install_command ]>>`; frozen installation is only valid after bootstrap has created the first lock. Commit the generated [% if package_manager == "npm" %]`package-lock.json`[% elif package_manager == "pnpm" %]`pnpm-lock.yaml`[% elif package_manager == "yarn" %]`yarn.lock`[% else %]`bun.lock`[% endif %]. Bootstrap does not require a database or application/provider credentials and does not initialize a database. Private dependency registries may still require their own authentication.

`scripts/jig setup` runs that same bootstrap, configures agent tooling, and verifies the harness with doctor. [% if db != "none" %]It leaves application database initialization to the explicit command below.[% endif %]

[% if db != "none" %]
Configure the application database, then initialize it explicitly:

```sh
[% if backend_language == "go" and go_backend_root != "." %]cp <<[ go_backend_root ]>>/.env.example <<[ go_backend_root ]>>/.env
[% else %]cp .env.example .env
[% endif %]```

Edit `DATABASE_URL` in that `.env` file (or export it), then run:

```sh
<<[ web_run_command ]>> database:setup
```

This runs `bash scripts/setup-database.sh`, which creates the database if missing and applies migrations. The role in `DATABASE_URL` must already exist. Use an existing database that the role can access and migrate, or a role already authorized to create the database. Setup never creates roles or grants permissions. Run the same command on fresh checkouts before starting the application.

[% endif %]
```sh
scripts/jig dev
```

Bootstrap commands, package scripts, and the database setup script are project-owned after init. Update and recopy preserve authored commands. For an older scaffold that initialized its database inside bootstrap, move that invocation to an explicit database setup script when adopting this workflow.

If a web check reports missing or stale dependencies, run `scripts/check-webapps.sh bootstrap` from this directory.

## Contracts

The public[% if admin_api_enabled %] and admin[% endif %] OpenAPI contract[% if admin_api_enabled %]s have separate [% if backend_language == "go" %]Huma exporters[% else %]Rust exporters[% endif %] and generated TypeScript packages[% else %] has a [% if backend_language == "go" %]Huma exporter[% else %]Rust exporter[% endif %] and generated TypeScript package[% endif %]. Regenerate [% if admin_api_enabled %]them[% else %]it[% endif %] atomically and verify committed output with:

```sh
<<[ web_run_command ]>> contract:generate
<<[ web_run_command ]>> contract:check
```

`contract:generate` stages all documents and clients before replacing committed files. `public:artifacts:check` proves that public frontend builds and the public server dependency graph contain no admin contract or client artifacts.

The harness checks the same boundaries without hiding them inside broad TypeScript checks: `scripts/check-webapps.sh application-contracts` checks contract freshness, while `scripts/check-webapps.sh public-artifacts` builds each non-admin frontend and checks its public artifacts.

[% if react_frontend_enabled %]
## Frontend linting

React applications share `eslint.config.shared.mjs`, which enables type-aware TypeScript linting, the React Hooks and TanStack Query recommended rules, and test-only Vitest and Testing Library rules. Package lint scripts reject warnings and stale ESLint suppressions. Use the cached package script during local iteration:

```sh
scripts/check-webapps.sh run-script <app-dir> lint:cached
```

Keep application-specific exceptions narrow. Product SPAs cannot import the admin API client, API client internals must be accessed through package entrypoints, components cannot import route modules, and modules under `src/domain` must remain independent of React and TanStack Router.

[% endif %]

[% if db == "postgres" %]
## Disposable PostgreSQL test

Run the database integration tests against a fresh PostgreSQL 18 container:

```sh
<<[ web_run_command ]>> test:postgres
```

The command binds PostgreSQL to a random loopback port and removes the container on exit.
[% if backend_language == "go" %]
It requires Docker and sets `TEST_DATABASE_URL` for the Go integration test, which checks database creation, application migrations, and generated queries against the disposable server.
[% else %]
Batter's fixture harness migrates one template database per migration fingerprint and gives each test its own clone, which it drops after the test even when the test fails. Set `POSTGRES_TEST_ADMIN_URL` to use an existing PostgreSQL 18 server whose role can create and drop databases instead.
[% endif %]
The tests never fall back to `DATABASE_URL`.

[% endif %]
[% if admin_api_enabled %]
## Admin boundary

The admin API is a separate binary and HTTP crate; it is not mounted by the public server. The generated admin binary explicitly uses `DenyAllAdminAuthorizer`, so admitted privileged requests return `401 Unauthorized` until you implement `AdminAuthorizer` and pass that implementation to `assemble` from `crates/<<[ package_name ]>>-admin-http` in the admin binary. The convenience `in_process` client uses an independent, approved lifecycle for tests, not production lifecycle wiring. Keep the admin listener private at the network/deployment layer as well.

Every admin response, including probes and rejections, carries `Cache-Control: no-store`, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`. Mutating admin requests must carry `Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin` and `x-admin-request: 1` before authorization runs; the admin panel's API client sends the marker, and browsers set Fetch Metadata. Non-browser admin clients must send both headers explicitly.

Do not add credentials, secrets, or sensitive examples to `openapi/admin.json`, and do not publish the admin client with public frontend or documentation artifacts.

[% endif %]
